# Valgrind suppressions for tests/ctgrind.rs — run via `verify.sh --ctgrind`.
#
# This file is a record of what has been examined and permitted, not a silencer.
# It contains exactly one entry, and it names exactly one function:
# `accept_or_reject`, the crate's accept/reject decision.
#
# # Why the entry names a function of its own
#
# Valgrind cannot match on source lines, so an entry can only name *functions* —
# and an entry naming a function permits every conditional jump anywhere in that
# function. An earlier revision suppressed `decrypt` and
# `decrypt_in_place_detached`, the two functions that contained the decision.
# That read as "the decision is permitted" and meant "any branch in these two
# functions is permitted": a secret-dependent branch added inside `decrypt`
# passed the check. Measured on a copy with a data-dependent loop over a poisoned
# key byte: 6 reports with no suppression file, 0 with it. The claim in the
# earlier header — that a *new function* branching on secret data would not be
# suppressed — was true, and it hid the case that mattered: a new branch inside a
# function that was already suppressed.
#
# The decision is now a function of its own, `#[inline(never)]`, shared by both
# decrypt entry points, and it is the only suppressed thing in the crate. Its
# whole body is the branch and the wipe, so the permission can be inspected:
#
#     if bool::from(auth_ok) { Ok(()) } else { zeroize_slice(buffer); Err(..) }
#
# The callers do not branch on the decision at all: it returns a `Result`, and
# `?` tests a discriminant that each arm writes as a constant, so neither entry
# point branches on secret-derived data. Two things *enforce* that instead of
# asserting it:
#
#  * `tools/ctgrind.sh` plants a secret-dependent branch inside `decrypt` and
#    inside `decrypt_in_place_detached` in a throwaway copy and requires that run
#    to fail; it reports a clean result only if that control was caught. See
#    `--selftest` there.
#  * `tools/ctgrind.sh` refuses to run at all unless every entry in this file
#    names `accept_or_reject`, so widening the suppression is caught by the check
#    that the suppression serves.
#
# # Why there is no "startup noise" group any more
#
# Earlier revisions enumerated the libtest, Rust-std and glibc startup reports
# (`free` and its inlined allocator, `__strlen_avx2`, `_int_realloc`,
# `__memchr_avx2`, `_dl_non_dynamic_init`, `pthread_getattr_np`, getopts, and the
# `setcc`-free libtest paths) with the exact frames each produced. Those frames
# are internal to three projects that all move independently, so the file kept
# breaking: entries generated against valgrind 3.24 stopped matching on 3.22,
# 3.26 and 3.27, and the Rust-std frames seen on a GitHub runner (thread-local
# lazy init, the mpmc channel, stack-overflow bookkeeping) appear nowhere in a
# local build at all. Every one of those failures was announced as "memcheck
# reported an unsuppressed secret-dependent branch", which is a finding in this
# crate — and none of them were.
#
# tools/ctgrind.sh now classifies instead: a report whose stack contains a
# `xchacha20_blake3_siv` frame fails the run, whether or not it also appears in
# this file, and anything else is reported as what it is — startup and teardown
# noise from other people's code — with a count so it is visible rather than
# silent. That is version-proof by construction, and it is strictly stronger for
# this crate: a secret-dependent branch *here* is caught wherever it is,
# including in code inlined into a test body, because the frame names come from
# DWARF.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The one permitted branch: the SIV accept/reject decision
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `accept_or_reject` ends in `if bool::from(auth_ok)`, where `auth_ok` comes from
# the tag, which comes from the secret key. That branch depends on secret-derived
# data and is unavoidable: SIV needs the plaintext before it can compute the tag,
# so decrypt-then-verify is inherent to the mode (the crate documents this). The
# decision reveals exactly one bit that is already public — whether this
# ciphertext authenticates.
#
# `--features hardened` compiles a second definition of the same function, with
# two recomputed gates and therefore two branches; both are inside it, and the
# entry below matches it too (v0 mangling encodes the path, not the signature, so
# both definitions carry this same name — only one of them exists in any given
# build).
#
# This must NOT be read as "the tag comparison is unchecked". The comparison
# *feeding* the branch is verified separately and mechanically:
# `constant_time_eq_does_not_branch_on_operands` in tests/ctgrind.rs poisons both
# operands, requires valgrind to report nothing from the comparison itself, and
# only unpoisons the resulting `Choice` before branching on it. The timing screen
# in tests/security.rs additionally checks position-independence end to end.

{
   siv-accept-or-reject-decision
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:_RNvCs*_20xchacha20_blake3_siv16accept_or_reject
}
